description: >
  Cursor rules for the DPVControl project (ESP32 + VESC DPV, REST API + webserver).
  These rules apply to all AI-assisted edits in this repo.
globs:
  - "**/*"
alwaysApply: true
---

This project involves a watercraft - a so-called Diver-Purpose Vehicle (DPV).
It is controlled by an ESP32, a VESC and other components.

Project information:
- Blog: https://bubtec.de/2024/10/29/diy-scooter-dpv-mit-aquazepp-und-dpvcontrol/
- GitHub: https://github.com/BubTec/DPVControl

Core project rules
- The code and comments are always in English.
- Always prefer small, isolated changes over large refactorings.
- For any non-trivial change, perform a full rebuild using PlatformIO.
- Follow the existing coding style (see .clang-format, .clang-tidy, .cpplint).
- Respect the existing module boundaries in src/ and test/.

API and webserver rules
- The project exposes a REST API documented in api-specification.yaml in the repo root.
- Whenever you change API behaviour in the webserver implementation (any C++ code in src/ that handles /api/... routes):
  - Ensure the OpenAPI file api-specification.yaml is updated accordingly.
  - Increment the API version in api-specification.yaml (and any related version field used by the web UI, e.g. in version.js, if applicable).
  - Keep request/response schemas in the spec exactly in sync with the actual JSON structures returned by the firmware.
- When adding a new endpoint or removing an existing one:
  - Update all relevant sections in api-specification.yaml (paths, schemas, examples, error responses).
  - Document authentication/authorization and possible error codes if applicable.

Logging rules
- If you add or extend serial logging:
  - Always use the existing logging/serial output helper function(s) already used in the project instead of introducing new ad-hoc Serial.print() calls.
  - Keep log messages concise and in English.
  - Avoid logging sensitive information (e.g. secrets, raw calibration values that should not be exposed).

Testing and automation rules
- This project uses PlatformIO tests and a pre-commit hook concept (see README).
- When any file under src/ or test/ is modified:
  - Run at least: `python -m platformio test -e native`.
  - For bigger or cross-cutting changes, prefer running the full test matrix via `run_all_tests.ps1` (present in the repo root).
  - When a shell/terminal is available in this workspace, execute these commands yourself rather than only suggesting them.
  - If tests fail, analyse and fix the problem before making further changes.
  - Do not propose a commit until all tests pass again.
- Never ask the user whether tests should be run; always assume that tests must be executed.
- When you fix a bug:
  - Check whether a test already exists that covers this bug.
  - If no such test exists, add a new test that reproduces the bug and verifies the fix.

API test coverage rules
- When modifying API endpoints (handlers for /api/status, /api/data, /api/trip-log, /api/settings, /api/settings/restore, etc.):
  - Ensure there is at least one dedicated test in the API test suite under test/ that exercises this endpoint.
    - If there is an existing API test module (e.g. a file with "api" in its name), extend it.
    - Otherwise, create a new API test module following the structure and naming conventions of existing tests.
  - For every endpoint, tests must cover:
    - At least one success case (valid input, expected JSON structure and HTTP status).
    - At least one error case (invalid or missing input, boundary conditions, or internal error).
  - JSON validation in tests must:
    - Check presence of required fields.
    - Validate value types.
    - If applicable, validate ranges or enums (e.g. state/status fields).
- If you change the JSON structure of any endpoint:
  - Update the corresponding tests under test/ accordingly.
  - Update api-specification.yaml so that the documented schema matches the new structure.

Mock and hardware abstraction rules
- The project controls real hardware (VESC, motor, sensors, LEDs, battery, etc.).
- Tests must never depend on real hardware.
- When adding new hardware-dependent functionality in src/:
  - Follow the existing pattern for hardware abstraction and mocking found in the test/ tree
    (e.g. mock_* headers and a central mock hardware implementation file, if present).
  - Add or extend mock_* files under test/ so that unit tests can simulate the new hardware behaviour.
  - Ensure that the tests for the new functionality use these mocks instead of real hardware APIs.
- When modifying existing hardware interaction code:
  - Keep hardware-specific code in dedicated modules (e.g. motor control, sensor reading, battery management), not mixed with business logic or API handlers.
  - Update corresponding mocks in test/ so that existing tests continue to reflect the new behaviour.

Test structure rules
- When adding new tests:
  - Place them in the appropriate subdirectory under test/ according to the existing structure (e.g. by domain: API, motor, sensors, state machine, etc.).
  - Include proper setup/teardown logic for shared state (e.g. global controller objects, mocks).
  - Use the Unity test framework conventions already used by PlatformIO tests (TEST_SETUP, TEST_TEAR_DOWN, etc.).
  - Follow existing naming conventions for test files and test cases (e.g. test_<module>.cpp, test_<feature>_<behaviour>).
- Tests must be deterministic:
  - No reliance on real time, random values, or external network/hardware.
  - If timing is relevant, use mocks or injected clocks.

Build and tooling rules
- The primary build system is PlatformIO (see platformio.ini).
- For significant changes (API, core control logic, hardware abstraction):
  - Perform a full rebuild using PlatformIO before suggesting a commit.
- Respect existing helper scripts in the repo root:
  - `run_all_tests.ps1`: used to run the complete test matrix.
  - `lint-check.ps1` and `check.cppcheck`: keep code compatible with the configured static analysis.
  - If your changes obviously break formatting or linting rules, adjust the code accordingly.

Web files embedding rules
- The webserver serves HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and images embedded directly in the firmware (not from LittleFS).
- All web files are stored in the `upload/` directory and converted to C++ headers by `embed_webfiles.py`.
- **IMPORTANT:** Whenever you modify any file in the `upload/` directory (HTML, CSS, JS, images, etc.):
  - You MUST run `python embed_webfiles.py` to regenerate the embedded files in `src/generated/`.
  - The generated files in `src/generated/` are auto-generated and should NOT be edited manually.
  - After running the embed script, perform a full rebuild with PlatformIO.
- The embed script uses binary-safe encoding (hex arrays) to prevent any string escaping issues.
- The `extra_script.py` runs the embed script automatically during PlatformIO builds, but:
  - It only runs on the first build after a clean.
  - If you change web files and click "Build" without "Clean", the script may not run.
  - **Best practice:** Always run `python embed_webfiles.py` manually after web file changes.
- Never modify files in `src/generated/` directly - they will be overwritten.

Git and commit message rules
- For every logical change you perform, propose a single, clear English commit message in the chat.
- Commit messages should:
  - Be written in imperative mood (e.g. "Add API test for /api/status error handling").
  - Describe both code and test changes (if tests were added or updated).
  - Mention "API" in the subject if an API-visible behaviour changed.
- Never run `git commit`, `git push`, `git pull`, or modify Git configuration/branches from tools, tasks, or scripts triggered by these rules.
- Only propose commit messages in the chat; all Git operations are performed manually by the user.
- Do not bundle unrelated changes into a single commit.

Continuous improvement of these rules
- If, during work on this project, you learn something that should be enforced systematically
  (for example, a new invariant, a problematic pattern, or a specific test requirement),
  propose an update to this rules file in the chat so the user can decide whether to adopt it.

Hardware Concurrency & LED Rules
- Single-Core LED Updates:
  - ALL NeoPixel/WS2812B LED updates (e.g., `strip.show()`, `setBar`) MUST be executed exclusively in the main `loop()` (Core 1).
  - NEVER call LED hardware functions from background FreeRTOS tasks (Core 0) to prevent RMT driver conflicts and corrupted LED data ("color mush").
- State-Based Decoupling:
  - Background tasks (e.g., VESC communication) must ONLY update global state variables (e.g., `batteryLevel`).
  - The visualization of this state must happen separately in the main loop based on these variables.
- Color Data Types:
  - Always use `uint32_t` (e.g., `0xFF0000`) for color values instead of `String` (e.g., `"#FF0000"`) to prevent heap fragmentation, reduce allocations, and avoid unnecessary string-to-color conversions.
